  "summary": "Weimar-era distorted sets, painted shadows and angular acting externalizing inner turmoil — Caligari to Nosferatu to Metropolis.",
  "description": "German Expressionism twisted cinema space into psychology: The Cabinet of Dr. Caligari's painted crooked walls, Nosferatu's skeletal shadows, Metropolis's vertical machine-city. Cheaper than location shooting, the style made distortion a dramatic tool; émigré directors carried it to Hollywood where it mutated into film noir and universal horror. Its graphic DNA — spiky silhouettes, extreme angles — still rules horror and goth visual culture.",
